A. Common setup (do this once)

Use Google Cloud Shell or a local terminal with Java and Maven installed. Check:

java -version
mvn -version
gcloud --version

Create the Maven web application folders:

mkdir -p ~/appengine-java/src/main/java/com/example
mkdir -p ~/appengine-java/src/main/webapp/WEB-INF
cd ~/appengine-java

Create pom.xml:

cat > pom.xml <<'EOF'
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
 xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
 xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <groupId>com.example</groupId>
  <artifactId>appengine-java</artifactId>
  <version>1.0</version>
  <packaging>war</packaging>
  <properties>
    <maven.compiler.release>17</maven.compiler.release>
    <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
  </properties>
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>jakarta.servlet</groupId>
      <artifactId>jakarta.servlet-api</artifactId>
      <version>6.0.0</version>
      <scope>provided</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
  <build>
    <finalName>app</finalName>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>3.13.0</version>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
EOF

Create the App Engine configuration:

cat > src/main/webapp/WEB-INF/appengine-web.xml <<'EOF'
<appengine-web-app xmlns="http://appengine.google.com/ns/1.0">
  <runtime>java17</runtime>
</appengine-web-app>
EOF

Common build, local testing and deployment

# Build the WAR file
mvn clean package

# A servlet needs a servlet container for local execution.
# Use your college's App Engine development server/launcher if provided,
# or deploy to App Engine after setting up the Google Cloud project.

gcloud app deploy
gcloud app browse

Note: App Engine Java deployment tools depend on the runtime and lab setup. Use the SDK/runtime version specified by your instructor. A servlet is not a standalone Java main() program.

